; ======================================================================
;  board.pi4 - THE ARDUINO UNO Q COMPILE TARGET.
;
;      pmfc ArduinoQ/Board/board.unoq -t unoq --entry-returns -o anvil-q.img
;      python tools/unoq_efi_wrap.py anvil-q.img anvil-q.efi \
;           --load-addr 0x70000000
;
;  The compile line used to carry --load-addr 0x70000000 --stack-addr
;  0x68000000 as well. It no longer needs to: this file DECLARES its
;  placement, at section 0 below, so the IDE - which embeds the compiler
;  and has no command line - builds the same bytes a script does.
;  unoq_efi_wrap.py still takes the load address, because it is a
;  separate program wrapping a finished image and has no way to read a
;  compiler statement out of the source.
;
;  This is the file Anvil/ARCHITECTURE.md names as the Q's counterpart to
;  RaspberryPi4/Board/board.pi4: it includes the Q's hardware backends and
;  the shared Anvil core, declares what this board can and cannot do, and
;  holds Main(). Same core bytes as the Pi 4. Different hardware. No fork.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  WHAT CHANGED SINCE Board/README.md SAID THIS COULD NOT BE WRITTEN
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  That note listed three blockers, and it was right about all three at
;  the time. Two are now cleared and the third is answered honestly:
;
;   1. "An interactive console ... there is no UartRead, no RxReady, and
;      no way to type at the board. The GENI SE4 UART is 1.8 V ... a
;      hardware blocker, not an afternoon's typing."
;      CLEARED, and not by getting a level shifter. Anvil runs on the Q as
;      a UEFI application with BOOT SERVICES STILL UP, so the firmware
;      publishes a console for us: ConOut and ConIn. The probe
;      (Examples/Diagnostics/anvilqconprobe.pi4, on the board 2026-09-02)
;      measured both alive. ArduinoQ/Board/qcon_q.unoq is the seam.
;   2. "The console/state globals ... none of it is reachable without (1)."
;      CLEARED by (1). Anvil/Core/state.pbi is included below, unchanged.
;   3. "Every capability backend ... on the Q every one of them is 0 today
;      ... the monitor it produced would refuse every hardware command it
;      has. That is a legitimate Anvil state and an honest one, but it is
;      not worth shipping ahead of a console to type into."
;      STILL TRUE, and now it IS worth shipping, because (1) means there
;      is a console to type into. Every #CAP_ below is 0 and the refusals
;      are the point: this is what the capability model is FOR.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE EXECUTION CONTEXT, MEASURED - this is what shapes the whole file
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  From anvilqcoreproof.pi4 and anvilqconprobe.pi4, both run on the board:
;
;    * A UEFI APPLICATION, boot services UP. Launched by systemd-boot
;      under U-Boot's EFI implementation as a one-shot entry; Debian stays
;      the DEFAULT, so the board always comes back. We never call
;      ExitBootServices, so the firmware's protocols stay legal all the
;      way to the reset.
;    * EL1. CurrentEL read back 0x4. Not EL2 - that single fact is why
;      #CAP_BOOT_EL1 is 0 and cannot be anything else from in here.
;    * The stack is UEFI's and MUST NOT BE MOVED. The Pi 4's Main() opens
;      by slamming SP to #MON_STACK because the firmware's boot stack is
;      in the way. Doing that here would destroy the stack the boot
;      manager expects back. This Main() therefore does NOT touch SP -
;      the single biggest structural difference from the Pi 4's Main().
;    * NO FRAMEBUFFER. LocateProtocol for EFI_GRAPHICS_OUTPUT_PROTOCOL
;      returns EFI_NOT_FOUND. There is no GOP, no screen command, no
;      screenshot, and ConOut's text goes to the firmware's serial stdout
;      (the 1.8 V SE4 UART) where nobody on this bench can read it.
;    * THE ESP IS WRITABLE. LoadedImage -> SimpleFileSystem -> Open ->
;      Write all return EFI_SUCCESS. That is the observable channel: the
;      session transcript lands at \EFI\anvil\anvil.log and is read over
;      SSH, and commands can be staged into \EFI\anvil\anvil.in the same
;      way. Those files are the Q's terminal.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  0. WHERE THIS IMAGE RUNS. THE FILE SAYS IT.              2026-09-02
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Until now these were pmfc flags and nothing else:
;
;    --load-addr 0x70000000 --stack-addr 0x68000000
;
;  which the IDE cannot pass, because it embeds the compiler and has no
;  command line. The source states them instead, so a Q build from the
;  IDE and a Q build from tools/build_unoq_anvil.sh are the same build.
;
;    LoadAddress  - inside the Q's confirmed-free low-bank DRAM block
;                   ($63900000-$7D9FFFFF). This image is position
;                   independent and UEFI may load it anywhere it likes;
;                   the number only has to be a real, usable address and
;                   to pass the target's load-base check.
;    BssAddress   - load + 8 MiB, inside the same confirmed-free block.
;                   IT WAS load + 512 KiB UNTIL 2026-09-08, which was
;                   the compiler's own default for this load address and
;                   was written out rather than left implicit. That
;                   number was a size cap on this program with 56,632
;                   bytes left in it: the image was 467,656 bytes and
;                   the next half-megabyte of code would have run into
;                   its own variables. The build would have refused,
;                   loudly, naming both ranges - which is better than
;                   the silent version the Pi 4 had, and is still a cap,
;                   and the ruling of that day is that this program has
;                   none. Eight megabytes is not a bigger cap being
;                   defended: CODE has to be contiguous with the load
;                   address and BSS does not, so the only thing this
;                   number has to be is far enough away that the size of
;                   the code is never the reason it moves. The Pi 4 made
;                   the same move to $08200000 on 2026-08-28 for the
;                   same reason.
;    StackAddress - a LINK-TIME value only. Anvil's Main() on the Q does
;                   NOT install it: the stack belongs to UEFI and this
;                   image has to return into the boot manager with that
;                   stack intact. See THE EXECUTION CONTEXT above.
;
;  --entry-returns is NOT one of these and stays a flag: it is not a
;  placement, it is a different contract with whatever launched the
;  image, and tools/build_unoq_anvil.sh still passes it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
LoadAddress  $70000000
BssAddress   $70800000
StackAddress $68000000

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE BUILD NUMBER (forum 586). An ordinary constant that CmdVersion()
;  prints; the trailing marker is what makes the IDE - and `pmfc
;  --bump-build` - raise it by one after a build that succeeded, writing
;  back that one line and nothing else.
;
;  THE Q COUNTS ITS OWN BUILDS. This board file is its own project, so it
;  carries its own number and the Pi 4's number does not move when this
;  one is built. The shared core in Anvil/Core carries no marker at all -
;  a library bumped by every project that includes it would be counting
;  nothing, which is what PMF-BLD-003 refuses by name.
;
;  Full write-up: the vault's Environment/Build number note, and the
;  header of BuildNumber.pbi in the compiler tree.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#ANVIL_BUILD = 18        ; pmf:build
#ANVIL_BUILD_DATE = 20260911   ; pmf:builddate
#ANVIL_BUILD_TIME = 062513     ; pmf:buildtime

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  1. THE Q'S HARDWARE BACKENDS.
;     Order matters: qefi_q defines the firmware plumbing every other Q
;     file calls; qcon_q defines the console name set (UartWrite and the
;     print router) that everything above it prints through; the timer and
;     info backends come before qstubs_q, which wraps the timer under the
;     names the core wants.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
XIncludeFile "ArduinoQ/Board/memmap_q.unoq"
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE DRIVER-MODULE CONTRACT AND THE SEAM REGISTRY, AS EARLY AS THEY CAN
;  GO - the same four files, in the same order, as the Pi 4 composition.
;  All four are constants and one table; none reads hardware or calls
;  anything else in this program. They are here rather than beside the
;  loader because a driver that a module can replace has to be able to
;  ask, inside its own file, whether one has.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Hal/seams.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Hal/module_format.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Hal/module_runtime.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/mod_registry.pbi"
XIncludeFile "ArduinoQ/Board/qefi_q.unoq"
XIncludeFile "ArduinoQ/Board/qcon_q.unoq"
XIncludeFile "ArduinoQ/Board/hwtimer_q.unoq"
XIncludeFile "ArduinoQ/Board/hwinfo_q.unoq"

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  2. THIS BOARD'S CAPABILITIES - what it offers Anvil's command families.
;
;  Every one is 0, and every one is 0 for a stated reason. Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi
;  is explicit that 0 covers both "the board has no such hardware" and "no
;  backend has been written for it yet", because from the operator's seat
;  those are the same fact and the same honest refusal.
;
;  This is NOT feature-gating to save space - hal.pi4 forbids that in as
;  many words, and nothing here is hidden that works. Each line says which
;  it is, so the next person knows whether they are writing a driver or
;  fighting the platform.
;
;  They must be declared BEFORE Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi and before every gated
;  command file, because that is where RequireCap reads them.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#CAP_STORAGE  = 1   ; YES, AND NOT THROUGH THE eMMC. Anvil still has no
                    ; driver for the Qualcomm SDHCI-MSM v5 controller
                    ; (mmc@4744000) and is not getting one here - but
                    ; #CAP_STORAGE does not ask for a block device, it
                    ; asks whether the core can reach FILES. It can:
                    ; Anvil runs as a UEFI application with boot services
                    ; up, so the firmware's own
                    ; EFI_SIMPLE_FILE_SYSTEM_PROTOCOL opens files by name
                    ; on the EFI System Partition this image was loaded
                    ; from. ArduinoQ/Board/hw_file_q.unoq is that backend.
                    ; load, save, settings, saveenv and boot all work.
                    ; fatls does NOT - enumerating a directory over that
                    ; protocol is a separate piece of work and is not
                    ; written, so HwFileCanList() answers 0 and the
                    ; command refuses in a sentence. #CAP_MMC stays 0
                    ; below: that family reads SECTORS, and there are
                    ; none of those to read here at all.
#CAP_NET      = 0   ; NO BACKEND. Wi-Fi is a WCBN3536A over SDIO and
                    ; there is no wired MAC Anvil reaches. The portable
                    ; IP/UDP/ARP stack has nothing to ride on.
#CAP_GPIO     = 1   ; REAL BACKEND. ArduinoQ/Lib/tlmm.unoq over the
                    ; QCM2290's TLMM at $00500000 - 127 lines, 0..126,
                    ; read and written by plain MMIO from here at EL1.
                    ; This line used to be 0 and read "NOT REACHABLE ...
                    ; Anvil has no pins here at all", which welded two
                    ; claims together, one true and one false.
                    ; TRUE, and it survives: the Arduino headers do NOT
                    ; reach the QCM2290 - they belong to the STM32U585 -
                    ; so HwGpioUserMax() returns -1 and `gpio` says so
                    ; rather than offering a header that is not there.
                    ; FALSE: that the SoC's own TLMM was out of reach. It
                    ; is not, and thirty of its lines carry on-board
                    ; functions the reserved list now names one by one.
#CAP_I2C      = 0   ; THE BACKEND IS WRITTEN AND THE BLOCK IS UNREACHABLE.
                    ; Not a missing driver: ArduinoQ/Lib/geni_i2c.unoq and
                    ; Board/hw_i2c_q.pi4 are complete, compiled into this
                    ; image, and gated off by this 0. What stops them is
                    ; the silicon's state under UEFI, and it was MEASURED
                    ; the hard way - see THE HANG below.
                    ;
                    ; The firmware question, which came first, is SETTLED
                    ; AND POSITIVE. A GENI engine is a programmable
                    ; sequencer and is an I2C master only if I2C protocol
                    ; firmware is loaded. Read from Linux over SSH,
                    ; GENI_FW_REVISION_RO says protocol 3 (I2C) on engines
                    ; 0 and 1, 2 (UART) on 2/3/4 and 1 (SPI) on 5. The
                    ; boot chain loads it and it lives in RAM inside the
                    ; engine. So the engines ARE I2C masters.
                    ;
                    ; THE HANG, 2026-09-02. A scripted session read the
                    ; QUPV3 wrapper's own version register at $04AC0004
                    ; with the `memory` command. The board did not fault
                    ; and did not reset - it STALLED, and needed a
                    ; physical power cycle. The transcript is flushed
                    ; after every command, so the log names the boundary
                    ; exactly: `version` completed, and nothing after it.
                    ;
                    ; On this SoC an MMIO access to a block whose clock is
                    ; off stalls the bus rather than returning $FFFFFFFF.
                    ; The wrapper is the FIRST thing in that aperture, so
                    ; it is not one engine that is gated - the whole QUP
                    ; wrapper is unclocked while Anvil runs. The
                    ; assumption that carrying the firmware console on
                    ; engine 4 must mean the wrapper is clocked was wrong.
                    ;
                    ; WHAT IT WOULD TAKE, in order: a GCC clock driver
                    ; (GCC is at $01400000) to ungate GCC_QUPV3_WRAP0_CORE
                    ; _CLK, _CORE_2X_CLK, _S0/_S1_CLK and the two AHB
                    ; clocks - which is reachable, it is register work.
                    ; Then the harder half: the QUP core clock is voted
                    ; for over the RPM interconnect, an SMD message to a
                    ; co-processor, and Anvil has no RPM IPC at all. Until
                    ; both exist this stays 0, and the driver waits.
#CAP_TOUCH    = 0   ; no touch panel controller in Anvil's reach on this
                    ; board. A 0 here is the capability model working: the
                    ; `touch` command's handler is a Raspberry Pi 4 board
                    ; file, so this build does not carry it at all, and the
                    ; shared help does not offer it.
#CAP_MMC      = 0   ; NO BACKEND. Same controller as #CAP_STORAGE; the
                    ; raw `mmc` family needs the same driver.
#CAP_USB      = 0   ; NO BACKEND. The host is a Synopsys DWC3
                    ; (qcom,qcm2290-dwc3, usb@4ef8800), not PCIe + VL805.
                    ; DWC3's host side is xHCI-register-compatible so some
                    ; of Lib/xhci may transfer later; none of it runs yet.
#CAP_PWM      = 0   ; NEITHER HALF IS REACHABLE. The group is a
                    ; modulated output AND the part's own temperature,
                    ; because the `fan` command needs both or neither.
                    ; The general-purpose PWM is behind the SAME QUPv3
                    ; wrapper that made #CAP_I2C zero above - the
                    ; measured hang, not a missing driver - and the
                    ; PMIC's LPG is over SPMI, which Anvil has no
                    ; controller for. The TSENS thermal block IS
                    ; reachable and simply has no driver. A software
                    ; toggle on a TLMM line would work and is
                    ; deliberately not offered: this board's Arduino
                    ; header belongs to the STM32U585 (see #CAP_GPIO), so
                    ; every line Anvil can drive here is an on-board
                    ; function and none of them is where a fan would be
                    ; wired. ArduinoQ/Board/qstubs_q.unoq carries the
                    ; absent answers; the reasoning is written out there
                    ; at greater length.
#CAP_BOOT_EL1 = 0   ; NOT POSSIBLE FROM HERE, and this one is not a
                    ; missing driver. UEFI runs this application at EL1 -
                    ; CurrentEL read 0x4 on the board. Installing an EL1
                    ; for a kernel requires being at EL2. On the Pi 4
                    ; Anvil runs at EL2 and can; here it cannot, and no
                    ; code inside the image changes that.

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE SEVEN GROUPS THE PAYLOAD ABI ADDED, 2026-09-04. ALL SEVEN ARE 0
;  HERE, HONESTLY.
;
;  Anvil/Hal/abi.pbi hands a payload a service table, and a payload is an
;  APPLICATION rather than a diagnostic - so it needs classes of hardware
;  the monitor's own command families never wanted. The Q has none of them
;  reachable from inside a UEFI application today, and saying so in seven
;  lines is what lets the SAME service table exist on both boards with the
;  same slot numbers, refusing where it must.
;
;  THAT IS THE POINT OF DECLARING THEM AT ALL rather than leaving them
;  undefined. A payload built for the Pi 4 and run here does not fault; it
;  asks SvcCapGet, is told 0, and renders a whole-sentence refusal. Which
;  is the capability model doing exactly what it was written for.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#CAP_CONSOLE  = 0   ; NO PIXEL SURFACE. The console here is qcon_q.pi4 - a
                    ; UEFI text protocol, characters and not pixels. There
                    ; is a framebuffer behind UEFI's GOP and no backend
                    ; reaches it, and the Adreno 702 is desk-only pending
                    ; a decision on the SMMU stream table.
; #CAP_TOUCH is declared once, further up this block, and it is 0 - no
; touch panel controller is in Anvil's reach on this board and there is
; no USB host to hang one off either (see #CAP_USB above). It is not
; repeated here; see the note in the Raspberry Pi 4 board file for why
; one name gets one declaration.
#CAP_GNSS     = 0   ; NO BACKEND and no receiver.
#CAP_UART     = 0   ; NO BACKEND. The QUP serial engines are there; the
                    ; console is the only one brought up, and it is UEFI's.
#CAP_SPI      = 0   ; NO BACKEND. SPI would be another QUP GENI mode on the
                    ; same wrapper geni_i2c.pi4 drives, and the QUP wrapper
                    ; at 0x04AC0000 is the PROVEN-UNCLOCKED block this
                    ; board's address-safety seam refuses by name.
#CAP_VEHLINK  = 0   ; NO BACKEND. This is the seam that replaced CAN on
                    ; 2026-09-04, when the engine port was given to a second
                    ; unit talking over Wi-Fi or LoRa. Carrying it here
                    ; needs a radio, and #CAP_NET is 0 on this board.
#CAP_RTC      = 0   ; NO BACKEND. UEFI has a runtime-services time protocol
                    ; and nothing in this image calls it.

; The board's identity for the payload ABI, one of Anvil/Hal/abi.pbi's
; #SVC_BOARD_* codes. INFORMATIONAL ONLY - a payload that branches on it
; has re-forked the thing the capability model exists to prevent.
#SVC_BOARD_ID = 2   ; #SVC_BOARD_UNOQ

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  3. THE HAL SEAM, then this board's REAL backends, then the stubs for
;     the groups still declared 0 above.
;
;     hal.pi4 must precede every gated command handler, and it must
;     precede the two hw_*_q backends as well: they return #HW_GPIO_* and
;     #HW_I2C_* codes that hal.pi4 defines.
;
;     Each driver library comes immediately before the backend that wraps
;     it, because a library never includes a library (house rule 10) - so
;     the MAIN file is the one place the order is stated:
;
;       Lib/tlmm.pi4      the QCM2290 pin controller  -> hw_gpio_q.pi4
;       Lib/geni_i2c.pi4  the QUP GENI I2C master     -> hw_i2c_q.pi4
;
;     geni_i2c.pi4 additionally needs HwTimerUs() from hwtimer_q.pi4,
;     which is already included in section 1 above - every wait in that
;     driver is bounded by it, so a bus with no interconnect vote times
;     out instead of hanging a board with no visible console.
;
;     qstubs_q.pi4 comes LAST of the three and no longer defines any
;     HwGpio* or HwI2c* name; two definitions of HwGpioCount() in one
;     image is a duplicate-symbol error waiting to be resolved in
;     whichever order the includes happen to land.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi"
XIncludeFile "ArduinoQ/Lib/tlmm.unoq"
XIncludeFile "ArduinoQ/Board/hw_gpio_q.unoq"
XIncludeFile "ArduinoQ/Lib/geni_i2c.unoq"
XIncludeFile "ArduinoQ/Board/hw_i2c_q.unoq"
; The Q's HwFile* backend - the file-level storage seam over UEFI's
; EFI_SIMPLE_FILE_SYSTEM_PROTOCOL. It is what makes #CAP_STORAGE = 1 above
; true, and it must precede Anvil/Core/settings.pbi, which calls it.
XIncludeFile "ArduinoQ/Board/hw_file_q.unoq"
XIncludeFile "ArduinoQ/Board/qstubs_q.unoq"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/wallclock.pbi"
; The Q's HwBoard* answers - identity, reset and MMU state - and then the
; payload ABI itself. abi.pi4 is CORE and unedited for this board: it
; names no chip, and the three groups the Q cannot back (console, net,
; crypto beyond SHA-256) are compiled out by their own #CAP_ declarations
; above, leaving those slots pointing at SvcUnimplemented.
;
; IT MUST COME BEFORE Anvil/Core/pmfboot.pbi, which is the one caller of
; BuildServiceTable().
XIncludeFile "ArduinoQ/Board/hw_board_q.unoq"
; This board's half of the driver-module contract. Before abi.pbi because
; SvcCapGet asks it whether the core reaches the two new seams.
XIncludeFile "ArduinoQ/Board/hw_mod_q.unoq"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Hal/abi_version.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Hal/abi.pbi"

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  4. THE SHARED ANVIL CORE, included unchanged. Not one of these files
;     is edited for the Q, and that is the whole point of the split.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/state.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/format.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/build_identity.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/rxbreak.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/crc.pbi"
; THE INPUT SEAM, AHEAD OF THE LINE READER THAT NOW SITS ON IT. Both are
; portable core and neither is edited for this board: this one has no
; touch surface, so the dispatcher's contact side is simply never fed,
; while the byte side carries the serial console exactly as before.
; They must follow state.pbi (gLine, #LINE_MAX) and qstubs_q.unoq
; (gLastCR), and must precede parse.pbi.
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/input_events.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/text_edit.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/parse.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/memrange.pbi"
; THE ADDRESS-SAFETY SEAM - the reason this board needed one, and the
; only board so far with a table to declare. The backend comes first and
; the core side that asks it second, the same order hw_gpio_q.pi4 and
; gpio_cmd.pi4 have. hw_addr_q.pi4 needs three things already included
; above: memmap_q.pi4 for the DRAM extent, hal.pi4 for the #HW_ADDR_*
; codes, and format.pi4 because it prints this board's table into `info`.
XIncludeFile "ArduinoQ/Board/hw_addr_q.unoq"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/addrsafe.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/argfmt.pbi"
; THE SETTINGS STORE, and the file commands over it. All four of these are
; new to this board and none of them is edited for it - they reach the
; medium only through the HwFile* seam above, which is the whole point of
; Anvil/ARCHITECTURE.md's split. settings.pi4 must follow hw_file_q.pi4;
; settings_cmd.pi4 and fs_cmd.pi4 must follow settings.pi4 and memrange.pi4.
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/settings.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/memcmd.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/uptime.pbi"
; hw_id_q - THIS BOARD'S IDENTITY (forum 575). The core's banner and
; `version` ask the HwId* seam what board this is, what processor it has,
; what its console is, what pmfc target built it and where its own image
; lives; this file is the answer. After qefi_q.pi4 (the loaded-image
; record), hwinfo_q.pi4 (HwInfoEl) and format.pi4 (PutAddr), all above,
; and before the two files that call it.
XIncludeFile "ArduinoQ/Board/hw_id_q.unoq"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/flow_cmd.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/gpio_cmd.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/i2c_cmd.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/mmc_cmd.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/usb_cmd.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/boot_cmd.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/fs_cmd.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/settings_cmd.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/time_cmd.pbi"
; sha256.pi4 is pure computation with no chip in it at all, which is why
; it lives in Anvil/Core rather than under a board. The bootloader below
; needs it: the hash check before a payload is entered is mandatory.
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/sha256.pbi"
; THE BOOTLOADER - the payload container, the `boot` family, and the
; persisted-boot-target countdown Main() runs before the prompt.
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/pmfboot.pbi"
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE DRIVER-MODULE LOADER, IDENTICAL TO THE Pi 4's. Every file here is
;  portable core: the container reader, the validator, the relocator, the
;  arena, the lifecycle, the manifest reader and the console. What this
;  board supplies differently is hw_mod_q.unoq above, which today says
;  there is no arena - so the whole of this compiles and links, `mod`
;  answers honestly, and nothing is executed from memory nobody reserved.
;
;  THAT IS THE BUILD-CLOSURE CLAIM AND ITS EXACT LIMIT: this proves the
;  core has no board-specific fork, and it does not prove a module loads
;  on Q silicon, which needs a Q.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/mod_container.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/mod_arena.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/mod_lifecycle.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/mod_manifest.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/mod_cmd.pbi"
; ... and its console side: the `boot` command word, the four boot.*
; settings, and BootFileWait(). Separate from the engine above so the
; engine can be built - and gated - without a line editor.
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/bootfile_cmd.pbi"
; The fan curve and the `fan` command. Both Core and both compiled here
; even though #CAP_PWM is 0: one command for every board, degrading
; through RequireCap, is the model - there is no second copy and no
; CompilerIf on a board name. fan.pi4 is pure arithmetic and needs
; nothing; fan_cmd.pi4 needs hal.pi4, the HwPwm*/HwTemp* answers in
; qstubs_q.pi4, settings.pi4 and bootfile_cmd.pi4 above it.
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/fan.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/fan_cmd.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/help.pbi"

; ======================================================================
;  CmdQInfo - the Q's `info`.
;
;  `info` is a per-board command on every Anvil target (the Pi 4's lives
;  in Board/mailbox_info.pi4 and asks the VideoCore mailbox). The Q has no
;  mailbox; its identity comes from architectural system registers, which
;  hwinfo_q.pi4 already reads, plus what the firmware told us.
; ======================================================================
Procedure CmdQInfo()
  PrintN("Arduino UNO Q - Qualcomm Dragonwing QRB2210 (QCM2290)")
  Print("  MIDR_EL1     ")
  PutHex16(RdMidr())
  If HwInfoIsQ() <> 0
    PrintN("   Qualcomm, Cortex-A53")
  Else
    PrintN("   (does not decode as a QCM2290 A53)")
  EndIf
  Print("  exception    EL")
  PrintDec(HwInfoEl())
  PrintN("   (UEFI runs this application here)")
  Print("  arch timer   ")
  PrintDec(TickHz())
  PrintN(" Hz")
  Print("  DRAM         ")
  PutAddr(HwInfoDramBase())
  Print(" to ")
  PutAddr(HwInfoDramTop() - 1)
  PrintNl()
  Print("  uptime       ")
  PrintDec(HwUptimeMs())
  PrintN(" ms since this image started")
  PrintN("  console      UEFI EFI_SIMPLE_TEXT_OUTPUT/INPUT_PROTOCOL")
  Print("    ConOut     ")
  PutHex16(gQConOut)
  Print("   ConIn ")
  PutHex16(gQConIn)
  PrintNl()
  Print("    keys read  ")
  PrintDec(gQKeysSeen)
  If gQInUsed <> 0
    Print("   staged script bytes ")
    PrintDec(gQInLen)
  Else
    Print("   no staged script")
  EndIf
  PrintNl()
  ; WHERE THE FIRMWARE PUT US. Printed because it is the number the
  ; monitor-overlap guard is built on: HitsMonitor() asks HwMonLo/HwMonHi,
  ; and on this board those are this extent. When it reads "not known",
  ; `boot` refuses every payload on purpose - so an operator meeting that
  ; refusal needs to be able to see WHY without reading source.
  Print("  image at     ")
  If QEfiImageKnown() = 1
    PutHex16(QEfiImageBase())
    Print(" .. ")
    PutHex16(QEfiImageBase() + QEfiImageSize() - 1)
    Print("   (")
    PrintDec(QEfiImageSize())
    PrintN(" bytes, from LoadedImage)")
  Else
    PrintN("not known - the firmware would not say where it loaded this")
    PrintN("               image, so boot refuses every payload rather than risk")
    PrintN("               placing one over the running monitor.")
  EndIf
  PrintN("  framebuffer  none - this firmware publishes no GOP, so there is")
  PrintN("               no screen on this board and no screenshot command.")
  ; AND NO DSI EITHER, SAID OUT LOUD RATHER THAN LEFT TO BE INFERRED.
  ; The Pi 4 gained a SECOND kind of screen on 2026-09-08: a DSI panel
  ; the monitor lights itself, out of its own memory, because that
  ; board's firmware owns no DSI display and never brings the block up.
  ; That is a Pi 4 seam and it does not come here - nothing on this part
  ; publishes a display controller, a display list or a pixel valve, and
  ; there is no connector to plug a panel into. A reader who saw "no
  ; GOP" above and knew about the panel would otherwise be left
  ; wondering whether it applied, and a seam that leaves that question
  ; open gets it answered wrongly one day.
  PrintN("               There is no DSI panel here either. This board has no")
  PrintN("               display controller of any kind, so the screen commands")
  PrintN("               are absent rather than refusing.")
  ; WHAT THIS BOARD SAYS ABOUT ADDRESSES. It goes in `info` because every
  ; refusal the address guard prints ends by sending the operator here,
  ; and because on this board it is the most useful thing the command
  ; says: it is the difference between "the monitor is being awkward" and
  ; "that block stalled this board on 2026-09-02 and here is the range".
  HwAddrReport()
  Print("  transcript   ")
  PrintDec(gQLogLen)
  PrintN(" bytes, flushed to \EFI\anvil\anvil.log on the ESP")
  If gQLogLost <> 0
    Print("  !! transcript overflowed - ")
    PrintDec(gQLogLost)
    PrintN(" bytes were dropped, so the log is incomplete.")
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  QBanner AND CmdQVersion ARE GONE, and their absence is the fix for
;  forum 575.
;
;  This board used to carry its own banner and its own `version`, because
;  the shared core stated the OTHER board's facts as literal prose. Run
;  unchanged here the core printed, on the first board run of this
;  monitor:
;
;      "PureMetal Forge - Anvil, the Raspberry Pi 4 serial monitor."
;      "The serial port is the PL011 at 00000000"
;      "The processor is in AArch64 at exception level 2."
;      "Built by pmfc for target pi4."
;
;  Four claims, all false here: this is not a Pi 4, there is no PL011
;  (the console is a UEFI protocol, and 00000000 is #UART_BASE having no
;  meaningful value to give it), the processor is at EL1 not EL2 -
;  measured, CurrentEL reads 0x4 - and this image was built `-t unoq`.
;  The last is the worst of the four: it is not vague branding but a
;  specific, checkable claim about how the image was produced, and
;  somebody diagnosing a bad image would go looking where it points.
;
;  The board-local copies made this board right and left the defect in
;  place, which the note here said out loud at the time: the core hard-
;  coding one board's facts is a real defect in the core, and a third
;  board would have inherited the same wrong identity again.
;
;  IT IS FIXED IN THE CORE NOW. The banner and `version` ask the board
;  through the HwId* seam (Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi), this board answers in
;  ArduinoQ/Board/hw_id_q.unoq, and there is exactly ONE banner and ONE
;  version command in the tree again. Main() calls Banner() and the
;  dispatcher calls CmdVersion(), the same two the Pi 4 calls.
;
;  DO NOT RE-ADD A BOARD-LOCAL BANNER. If a board needs to say something
;  the core does not print, that is a row in the HwId* seam - which is
;  what HwIdSayConsole() and HwIdSayImage() already are.
; ======================================================================

; ======================================================================
;  CmdQReset - the Q's `reset`.
;
;  On the Pi 4 this is a hardware reset through the watchdog. Here the
;  right thing is a UEFI cold reset, which returns the board to Debian
;  (the default boot entry). The transcript is flushed FIRST: a reset that
;  discarded the session record would make the one observable channel on
;  this board useless exactly when it matters.
; ======================================================================
Procedure CmdQReset()
  PrintN("resetting: handing the board back to Debian (the default boot entry).")
  UartDrain()
  QLogFlush()
  QEfiReset()
  ; ResetSystem does not return. If it somehow does, say so rather than
  ; carrying on as though the command worked.
  PrintN("!! ResetSystem returned, which it is not supposed to do. The board")
  PrintN("   is still here and still running Anvil. Nothing else was changed.")
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  CmdQExit - leave Anvil cleanly.
;
;  This command exists on the Q and on no other board, and it is not a
;  convenience. Anvil is a ONE-SHOT UEFI boot entry here: the operator has
;  to be able to end the session and get Debian back, and a staged script
;  needs a way to say "that is all". Without it the only exit is the idle
;  deadman in qcon_q.pi4 or pulling the power.
; ======================================================================
Procedure CmdQExit()
  gQExitWanted = 1
  gQExitReason = 3
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  Main() - the Q's entry point.
;
;  UEFI enters at x0 = ImageHandle, x1 = SystemTable, which pmfc's
;  --entry-returns _start preserves into Main(). Those two registers are
;  captured in the FIRST instructions, before anything can clobber them.
;
;  The boot order is the shared one Anvil/ARCHITECTURE.md fixes -
;  banner -> devices -> prompt - with "devices" honestly empty on this
;  board. It does NOT move the stack: see the header.
; ======================================================================
Procedure Main()
  Define c.i
  Define hz.i
  Define ih.i
  Define st.i
  Define ndev.i
  Define nabs.i

  ; ---- capture x0/x1 before anything else runs -----------------------
  ASM
    adrp x9, global_gqih
    add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_gqih
    str  x0, [x9]
    adrp x9, global_gqst
    add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_gqst
    str  x1, [x9]
  EndASM
  ih = gQIh
  st = gQSt

  ; ---- bring the firmware up. If the SystemTable is not valid there is
  ;      no console, no ESP and nowhere to complain to, so the only
  ;      correct action is to return to the boot manager, which boots
  ;      Debian. Returning 1 marks it as a failure for anything watching.
  If QEfiInit(ih, st) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  QConInit()

  UartInit(115200)

  If TimerInit() = 0
    PrintN("!! the architectural counter is not running - every timeout in")
    PrintN("   this monitor is guesswork. Transfers may not time out.")
  EndIf
  gBootTicks = Ticks()
  WallClockInit()

  hz = TickHz()
  If hz <= 0 Or hz > 1000000000
    hz = 19200000                ; the QCM2290's counter, as a fallback
  EndIf
  gToTicks = hz + hz

  gBad = 0
  gFail = 0
  gHaveEntry = 0
  gMbx = 0

  Banner()

  ; ---- devices. The shared boot order calls for bringing the board's
  ;      buses up here. Saying once at boot which groups exist is better
  ;      than letting the operator discover it one refused command at a
  ;      time.
  ;
  ;      ***DERIVED FROM THE #CAP_ FLAGS, NEVER RESTATED AS PROSE.*** This
  ;      used to be three hardcoded sentences asserting every group was 0,
  ;      and on 2026-09-02 it went on asserting it after the GPIO backend
  ;      landed and #CAP_GPIO became 1. The board printed "Every hardware
  ;      group on this board is declared 0 - storage, net, gpio, i2c, ..."
  ;      from an image whose gpio backend was real.
  ;
  ;      That is exactly what vault rule 3 exists to stop: a specific,
  ;      checkable claim about the machine, stated confidently, and wrong.
  ;      A diagnostician reading that banner stops looking. The flags are
  ;      the truth, so the flags are what is printed - there is only one
  ;      statement of it now, so the two cannot drift apart again.
  Print("devices:")
  ndev = 0
  If #CAP_STORAGE
    Print(" storage")
    ndev = ndev + 1
  EndIf
  If #CAP_NET
    Print(" net")
    ndev = ndev + 1
  EndIf
  If #CAP_GPIO
    Print(" gpio")
    ndev = ndev + 1
  EndIf
  If #CAP_I2C
    Print(" i2c")
    ndev = ndev + 1
  EndIf
  If #CAP_MMC
    Print(" mmc")
    ndev = ndev + 1
  EndIf
  If #CAP_USB
    Print(" usb")
    ndev = ndev + 1
  EndIf
  If #CAP_BOOT_EL1
    Print(" booti")
    ndev = ndev + 1
  EndIf
  If ndev = 0
    Print(" none")
  EndIf
  PrintN(".")

  Print("absent:")
  nabs = 0
  If #CAP_STORAGE = 0
    Print(" storage")
    nabs = nabs + 1
  EndIf
  If #CAP_NET = 0
    Print(" net")
    nabs = nabs + 1
  EndIf
  If #CAP_GPIO = 0
    Print(" gpio")
    nabs = nabs + 1
  EndIf
  If #CAP_I2C = 0
    Print(" i2c")
    nabs = nabs + 1
  EndIf
  If #CAP_MMC = 0
    Print(" mmc")
    nabs = nabs + 1
  EndIf
  If #CAP_USB = 0
    Print(" usb")
    nabs = nabs + 1
  EndIf
  If #CAP_BOOT_EL1 = 0
    Print(" booti")
    nabs = nabs + 1
  EndIf
  If nabs = 0
    Print(" nothing")
  EndIf
  PrintN(".")
  PrintN("  Type help for what works, or info for why an absent one is absent.")
  PrintNl()

  ; THE DRIVER MODULES, before the boot countdown, for the Pi 4's reason:
  ; a payload entered with the service table is entitled to find the seams
  ; a loaded module fills, so loading them afterwards would make the same
  ; medium produce two different machines depending on timing. On this
  ; board it currently does nothing and prints nothing - hw_mod_q.unoq
  ; declares no arena - and `mod` says so in one sentence when asked.
  ModBoot()

  ; THE PERSISTED BOOT TARGET. Anvil/Core/pmfboot.pbi's BootFileWait()
  ; reads boot.file out of SETTINGS.TXT on the ESP, counts down, and boots
  ; the container it names - or returns at once when nothing is armed.
  ;
  ; THERE IS NO BootWait() CALL BEFORE IT, and that is the one difference
  ; from the Pi 4's Main(). BootWait takes the DRAM autoboot record, and
  ; this board has nowhere that survives a reset to keep one - AutoEntry()
  ; in qstubs_q.pi4 correctly answers 0 always. The persisted target is
  ; therefore the only automatic boot here, which is also why it matters
  ; more here: a settings file on the ESP is the only memory this board
  ; has across a power cycle.
  ;
  ; IT RUNS BEFORE THE STAGED SCRIPT IS ANNOUNCED, deliberately, so that
  ; the order an operator reads in the transcript is the order things
  ; happened in: devices, then the boot countdown, then the prompt and
  ; whatever is typed at it.
  BootFileWait()
  TimeBoot()

  ; THE FAN POLICY, after the settings and before the prompt - the same
  ; place and the same reason as on the Pi 4. On this board #CAP_PWM is
  ; 0, so FanBoot() resets the curve to its defaults and returns without
  ; touching a pin or printing a line; it is called anyway so that the
  ; two boards' Main() differ in what the HARDWARE can do and not in
  ; which core routines they remember to call.
  FanBoot()

  If gQInUsed <> 0
    Print("input: a staged command script was found on the ESP, ")
    PrintDec(gQInLen)
    PrintN(" bytes.")
    PrintN("  It is being fed to this prompt as if typed. Keystrokes from the")
    PrintN("  firmware console are still accepted and take effect immediately.")
    PrintNl()
  EndIf

  ; ====================================================================
  ;  THE COMMAND LOOP.
  ;
  ;  This is the Pi 4's loop (RaspberryPi4/Board/board.pi4:1504) with the
  ;  handlers this board actually includes, and nothing else. It is
  ;  written out rather than called because there is no CommandLoop() in
  ;  the core to call - on every Anvil board the loop lives inline in the
  ;  board's Main(), and the Pi 4's is the reference.
  ;
  ;  "pmf> " is load-bearing: five characters, trailing space, no newline.
  ;  Host tools match on it. Do not tidy it.
  ; ====================================================================
  Repeat
    Print("pmf> ")
    UartDrain()

    ; gQTyping brackets the line editor and NOTHING else. Outside these
    ; two lines the staged script is invisible to UartReadReady(), which
    ; is what stops Anvil/Core/rxbreak.pbi's break detector from reading
    ; the next queued command as "the operator pressed a key" and
    ; aborting every command that prints. See the long note on gQTyping
    ; in ArduinoQ/Board/qcon_q.unoq - this cost a board run to find.
    gQTyping = 1
    ReadLine()
    gQTyping = 0

    ; The deadman in qcon_q.pi4, or the `exit` command, ends the session.
    ; It is checked BEFORE the line is dispatched so a line the deadman
    ; completed with a synthetic CR is not run as a command.
    If gQExitWanted <> 0
      Break
    EndIf

    OutBreakArm()
    gPos = 0
    SkipSpace()
    gWordAt = gPos
    c = gLine[gPos] & $FF
    SkipWord()
    gWordLen = gPos - gWordAt

    gMemWidth = 0
    ; THE SAFETY OVERRIDE AGES HERE, beside the gMemWidth reset and for
    ; the same stated reason: state must not leak from one command into
    ; the next. `force <address>` arms one address for the NEXT command
    ; line and this ends it - used or not. On THIS board that is not a
    ; nicety: the thing being armed is an access that stalls the
    ; processor. See Anvil/Core/addrsafe.pbi.
    ForceAge()
    If gWordLen >= 3 And gLine[gWordAt + gWordLen - 2] = 46      ; 46 = '.'
      Define suf.i
      suf = gLine[gWordAt + gWordLen - 1] & $FF
      If suf = 98 Or suf = 66                                    ; b / B
        gMemWidth = 1
      ElseIf suf = 119 Or suf = 87                               ; w / W
        gMemWidth = 2
      ElseIf suf = 108 Or suf = 76                               ; l / L
        gMemWidth = 4
      EndIf
      If gMemWidth <> 0
        gWordLen = gWordLen - 2                                  ; hide ".x"
      EndIf
    EndIf

    If c = 0
      ; An empty line reprints the prompt and does nothing else.
    ElseIf WordIs("memory") <> 0 Or WordIs("dump") <> 0 Or WordIs("md") <> 0 Or LetterIs(109) <> 0
      CmdMem()
    ElseIf WordIs("write") <> 0 Or WordIs("poke") <> 0 Or LetterIs(119) <> 0
      CmdWrite()
    ElseIf WordIs("compare") <> 0 Or WordIs("cmp") <> 0
      CmdCompare()
    ElseIf WordIs("copy") <> 0 Or WordIs("cp") <> 0
      CmdCopy()
    ElseIf WordIs("crc32") <> 0 Or WordIs("checksum") <> 0
      CmdCrc32()
    ElseIf WordIs("fill") <> 0 Or WordIs("mw") <> 0
      CmdFill()
    ElseIf WordIs("mm") <> 0
      CmdMm()
    ElseIf WordIs("nm") <> 0
      CmdNm()
    ElseIf WordIs("mtest") <> 0
      CmdMtest()
    ElseIf WordIs("base") <> 0
      CmdBase()
    ; force - arm one address this board refused, for the next command
    ; line only. Whole word, no alias and no abbreviation: it is the
    ; confirmation for an access that can stall this processor, and a
    ; short way to type it would be the accident it exists to prevent.
    ElseIf WordIs("force") <> 0
      CmdForce()
    ElseIf WordIs("run") <> 0 Or WordIs("go") <> 0 Or LetterIs(103) <> 0
      CmdGo()
    ElseIf WordIs("booti") <> 0
      CmdBooti()
    ElseIf WordIs("bootm") <> 0
      CmdBootm()
    ; boot - THE BOOTLOADER. `boot <name>` reads a payload container off
    ; the ESP, takes the load address, entry point and SHA-256 out of it,
    ; places the image, proves it and enters it, with no address typed.
    ; `boot mem <addr>` does the same for one already in DRAM.
    ElseIf WordIs("boot") <> 0
      CmdBoot()
    ; THE FILE COMMANDS. New on this board and unedited for it: load and
    ; save ride the HwFile* seam over UEFI, and fatls refuses in a
    ; sentence because this board cannot enumerate a directory.
    ElseIf WordIs("load") <> 0 Or WordIs("fatload") <> 0 Or LetterIs(102) <> 0
      CmdFat()
    ElseIf WordIs("save") <> 0
      CmdSave()
    ElseIf WordIs("fatls") <> 0 Or WordIs("ls") <> 0 Or WordIs("dir") <> 0
      CmdFatls()
    ; THE SETTINGS STORE and U-Boot's names for it. SETTINGS.TXT lives at
    ; \EFInvil on the ESP and survives a power cycle, which is what a
    ; persisted boot target needs.
    ElseIf WordIs("settings") <> 0
      CmdSettings()
    ElseIf WordIs("printenv") <> 0
      CmdEnvPrint()
    ElseIf WordIs("setenv") <> 0
      CmdEnvSet()
    ElseIf WordIs("saveenv") <> 0
      CmdSettingsSave()
    ElseIf WordIs("env") <> 0
      CmdEnv()
    ElseIf WordIs("time") <> 0
      CmdTime()
    ElseIf WordIs("uptime") <> 0 Or WordIs("up") <> 0
      CmdUptime()
    ElseIf WordIs("echo") <> 0
      CmdEcho()
    ElseIf WordIs("sleep") <> 0
      CmdSleep()
    ElseIf WordIs("version") <> 0 Or WordIs("ver") <> 0
      CmdVersion()
    ElseIf WordIs("setexpr") <> 0
      CmdSetexpr()
    ElseIf WordIs("gpio") <> 0
      CmdGpio()
    ElseIf WordIs("i2c") <> 0
      CmdI2c()
    ElseIf WordIs("fan") <> 0
      CmdFan()
    ; mod - the driver-module loader. Portable core; this board's own
    ; hw_mod_q.unoq is what says whether an arena exists here yet.
    ElseIf WordIs("mod") <> 0
      CmdMod()

    ElseIf WordIs("mmc") <> 0
      CmdMmc()
    ElseIf WordIs("usb") <> 0
      CmdUsb()
    ; map - the memory map as the running image measures it. Core, chip-free,
    ; no capability: every board can say where it put itself. A whole word
    ; only; m is the memory dump. See CmdMap in Anvil/Core/memcmd.pbi for
    ; why it is a command of its own and not a section of `info`.
    ElseIf WordIs("map") <> 0
      CmdMap()

    ElseIf WordIs("info") <> 0 Or WordIs("board") <> 0 Or LetterIs(105) <> 0
      CmdQInfo()
    ElseIf WordIs("reset") <> 0 Or WordIs("reboot") <> 0 Or LetterIs(114) <> 0
      CmdQReset()
    ElseIf WordIs("exit") <> 0 Or WordIs("quit") <> 0
      CmdQExit()
    ElseIf WordIs("help") <> 0 Or WordIs("commands") <> 0 Or LetterIs(104) <> 0 Or LetterIs(63) <> 0
      Help()
    Else
      Define k.i
      Print("? this monitor does not know the command ")
      k = 0
      While k < gWordLen And k < 24
        UartWrite(gLine[gWordAt + k] & $FF)
        k = k + 1
      Wend
      PrintN(", so nothing was done.")
      PrintN("  Type help, or just a question mark, for the list of commands.")
      PrintN("  Every command is a whole word; the old single letters still work.")
    EndIf

    ; The transcript is flushed after EVERY command, not once at the end.
    ; This is the only channel anyone on this bench can read, and a
    ; session that ends in a hang or a fault must still leave everything
    ; up to the last completed command on disk.
    QLogFlush()

    If gQExitWanted <> 0
      Break
    EndIf
  ForEver

  ; ---- shutdown ------------------------------------------------------
  PrintNl()
  If gQExitReason = 2
    PrintN("no input for 45 seconds and no console attached, so Anvil is")
    PrintN("standing down rather than hanging a board nobody can see.")
  ElseIf gQExitReason = 3
    PrintN("exit: closing the session.")
  Else
    PrintN("the staged command script ended.")
  EndIf
  Print("transcript: ")
  PrintDec(gQLogLen)
  PrintN(" bytes at \EFI\anvil\anvil.log")
  PrintN("handing the board back to Debian.")
  UartDrain()
  QLogFlush()

  QEfiReset()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Main()
